  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nEARLY THIS MORNING--EARLY FRIDAY...While chances appear low at this \ntime and most locations will remain thunderstorm-free, can't rule \nout a few isolated thunderstorms through this period, mainly \nalong/west of I-135. Slight chances this morning are tied to weak \n850-700mb warm/moist advection emanating from a possible MCV \napproaching over western KS. For later this afternoon, the \nairmass looks unstable and weakly capped which may support an \nisolated thunderstorm or two. For late tonight through early \nFriday, another round of weak 850-700mb warm/moist advection may\nsupport additional slight thunderstorm chances. In all cases, \nweak deep layer shear will prevent storm organization and \nseverity, although hefty downdraft CAPE may support an isolated \nsevere wind gust, especially late this afternoon-evening.\n\nSUNDAY EVENING--MID TO LATE NEXT WEEK...The upper ridge finally \nshifts east from the southwest to the south-central and southeast \nCONUS by this weekend, as various shortwaves gradually dig across \nthe northern and western CONUS. This pattern change should allow a \nweak cold front to move in from the north and stall across the \nregion, with low chances for off-and-on thunderstorms Sunday evening \nthrough mid to late next week, especially across the northwest half \nof Kansas. \n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nDeterministic and ensemble consensus is in good agreement \nsurrounding a prolonged period of triple digit to near triple digit \nheat from this weekend through much of next week (and possibly \nbeyond), as an anomalous southwest CONUS upper ridge shifts east \nover the south-central and southeast CONUS. The longevity of this \nheat wave will be determined by how far south a stalled frontal zone \nreaches, which at this time looks to remain mainly over the \nnorthwest half of Kansas. Some relief amidst this incoming heat wave \nwill be lower dewpoints (thinking NBM/forecasted dewpoints may be a \nbit too rich given global model consensus) and breezy/gusty south to \nsouthwest winds aiding in evaporative effects on skin.",
